docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/tasks/T82.md:12:This task delivers two test modules that prove the daemon survives the failure modes Phase 11 names explicitly: recovery from a partial-write or crash, and handling of corrupted event payloads. Spec §Storage Design requires event-log compaction snapshots to be "versioned and independently readable so the daemon can bootstrap from a snapshot without replaying the full history"; spec §3 makes the event log replayable; spec §Phase 11 deliverables list "recovery tests" and "corrupted-event handling" separately. Without this suite, a single corrupted event payload could brick the daemon and a partial snapshot would silently lose history — both unacceptable per spec §Non-Negotiable Product Properties ("every background consolidation pass is recoverable, replayable, and observable").
docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/tasks/T82.md:37:1. Read spec §Storage Design (event log compaction snapshot, versioned format), §3 Event Log (append-only, replayable), §Phase 2 deliverables (corruption handling), §Phase 11 deliverables (recovery + corrupted-event), and §Non-Negotiable Product Properties so the recovery and corruption invariants are pinned.
docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/tasks/T49.md:12:This task implements the second verification check from spec §Verification Engine: when a memory's evidence captured an exact text span (a byte range or `(start_line, end_line)`) at the time of creation, verification must confirm the current file content at that span still matches what was captured. If it does not, the verifier emits a `MarkStale` proposal (the assertion may still be true but the citation is stale) or `MarkInvalidated` (the evidence is gone). This is the difference between "the file still exists" (T48) and "what the memory said about the file is still backed by the same bytes." It is the load-bearing mechanism for the spec's §Stale Memory Leakage risk control.
docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/tasks/T49.md:40:7. Emit the verdict as a `MarkStale` proposal through the T39 runtime (never direct memory writes), carrying the structured reason payload.
docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/tasks/T49.md:57:- Single source of truth: span validation routes through `VerifierCore` (T48) and emits proposals through T39; no parallel memory writes; range reads go through the existing storage layer.
