docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/tasks/T39.md:1:# T39 — Phase 6 — Consolidation job runtime + proposal model (no silent writes)
docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/tasks/T39.md:7:**Spec anchor:** [§Consolidation Engine](../../2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-plan.md#6-consolidation-engine), [§Phase 6: Consolidation Engine](../../2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-plan.md#phase-6-consolidation-engine), [§Storage Design](../../2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-plan.md#storage-design)
docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/tasks/T39.md:12:This task stands up the consolidation engine's runtime kernel and the proposal model on which every later Phase 6 job depends. It introduces the `consolidation_jobs` table from spec §Storage Design, the bounded job queue, and a strict invariant that no consolidation job — deterministic or LLM-driven — writes durable memory directly. Every job emits a `ConsolidationProposal` record that requires explicit `apply` or `reject` before any memory state changes. Without this kernel, the LLM-driven jobs in T42 would be free to silently mutate memory, violating spec §Non-Negotiable Product Properties and the §Opaque Memory Mutation risk control.
docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/tasks/T39.md:18:> "each LLM-driven job produces a proposal record, not a direct write; proposals require explicit apply or reject before altering durable memory"
docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/tasks/T39.md:22:> "Every background consolidation pass is recoverable, replayable, and observable." (§Non-Negotiable Product Properties)
docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/tasks/T39.md:30:  - `daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/memory/` — current memory store API; proposals must wrap writes through the existing store rather than bypass it.
